{
  "content": "{\n  \"checks\": [\n    {\n      \"target\": 0,\n      \"draft_quote\": \"MIT 6.0001/600 第一讲先介绍课程目标（概念、编程技能、通过 psets 解决问题）并强调练习\",\n      \"source_quote\": \"There's three big things we want to get out of this course. The first thing is the knowledge of concepts, which is pretty much true of any class that you'll take. The class will teach you something through lectures. Exams will test how much you know. This is a class in programming. The other thing we want you to get out of it is programming skills. And the last thing, and I think this is what makes this class really great, is we teach you how to solve problems. And we do that through the psets.\",\n      \"finding\": \"Summary accurately reflects the three course goals and practice emphasis.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"target\": 0,\n      \"draft_quote\": \"计算机在明确指令下执行极快速的计算并存储结果\",\n      \"source_quote\": \"Fundamentally, it does two things. One, performs calculations. It performs a lot of calculations. Computers these days are really, really fast, a billion calculations per second is probably not far off. It performs these calculations and it has to store them somewhere. Right? Stores them in computer memory.\",\n      \"finding\": \"Summary accurately captures the two fundamental computer functions.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"target\": 0,\n      \"draft_quote\": \"讲座用陈述性知识与命令式知识、平方根算法、固定程序与存储程序架构、图灵原语等说明计算的基本原理\",\n      \"source_quote\": \"The first type of knowledge is declarative knowledge. And those are things like statements of fact. ... Imperative knowledge is the recipe, or the how-to, or the sequence of steps. ... Here's a well-known algorithm. To find the square root of a number x ... Historically, there were two different types of computers. Originally, there were these things called fixed-program computers. ... That's when stored-program computers came into play. ... Alan Turing, who was a really great computer scientist, he showed that you can compute anything using the six primitives.\",\n      \"finding\": \"Summary accurately lists the main conceptual topics.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"target\": 0,\n      \"draft_quote\": \"并转入 Python 基础，介绍对象与类型、表达式与运算符、打印、赋值与内存绑定，以及程序单一含义与英语歧义的区别\",\n      \"source_quote\": \"Now we're going to start talking about Python. And in Python, we're going to come back to this, everything is an object. ... All objects in Python are going to have a type. ... Once you have objects, you can combine objects and operators to form these expressions. ... To print things out, you use the print command. ... To save a value to a variable name, you use the equal sign. ... But in programming languages, the program that you write, the set of instructions that you write, only has one meaning.\",\n      \"finding\": \"Summary accurately covers the Python basics and the single meaning vs English ambiguity distinction.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"target\": 0,\n      \"draft_quote\": \"最后预告下一讲将加入控制流\",\n      \"source_quote\": \"Next lecture, we're going to talk about adding control flow to our programs, so how do you tell the computer to do one thing or another?\",\n      \"finding\": \"Summary accurately notes the next lecture topic.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"target\": 1,\n      \"draft_quote\": \"计算机根本上做两件事：执行大量计算并把结果存入计算机内存；现在计算速度极快（每秒十亿次计算“可能差不多”），数百 GB 存储也不罕见。\",\n      \"source_quote\": \"Fundamentally, it does two things. One, performs calculations. It performs a lot of calculations. Computers these days are really, really fast, a billion calculations per second is probably not far off. It performs these calculations and it has to store them somewhere. Right? Stores them in computer memory. So a computer also has to remember results. And these days, it's not uncommon to find computers with hundreds of gigabytes of storage.\",\n      \"finding\": \"Accurate.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"target\": 1,\n      \"draft_quote\": \"计算分两类：语言内置的原始计算（加、减、乘等）和程序员组合这些原始类型定义的新计算。\",\n      \"source_quote\": \"The kinds of calculations that computers do, there are two kinds. One are calculations that are built into the language. These are the very low level types of calculations, things like addition, subtraction, multiplication, and so on. And once you have a language that has these primitive calculation types, you, as a programmer, can put these types together and then define your own calculations. You can create new types of calculations.\",\n      \"finding\": \"Accurate.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"target\": 1,\n      \"draft_quote\": \"讲座反复强调：计算机只知道你告诉它的事，只做你告诉它做的事；它不神奇、没有心智，只是快速执行计算。\",\n      \"source_quote\": \"I think, one thing I want to stress-- and we're going to come back to this again during this entire lecture, actually-- is computers only know what you tell them. Computers only do what you tell them to do. They're not magical. They don't have a mind. They just know how to perform calculations really, really quickly.\",\n      \"finding\": \"Accurate.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"target\": 2,\n      \"draft_quote\": \"陈述性知识是事实陈述，例如“下课前会有人获奖”（奖品是 Google Cardboard），或“x 的平方根是 y，使得 y*y=x”；计算机不知道如何处理这种陈述本身。\",\n      \"source_quote\": \"The first type of knowledge is declarative knowledge. And those are things like statements of fact. ... So a statement of fact for today's lecture is, someone will win a prize before class ends. And the prize was a Google Cardboard. ... An example of declarative knowledge is the square root of a number x is y such that y times y is equal to x. That's just a statement of fact It's true. Computers don't know what to do with that.\",\n      \"finding\": \"Accurate.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"target\": 2,\n      \"draft_quote\": \"命令式知识是配方、做法或步骤序列；例如 Ana 按步骤打开 Anaconda IDE，用 Python 的 random.randint 在 16 到 272 之间抽到编号 75，再在回应者名单中找到该编号并选中 Lauren Z-O-V。\",\n      \"source_quote\": \"Imperative knowledge is the recipe, or the how-to, or the sequence of steps. ... I'm going to open my IDE. ... The IDE that we're using in this class is called Anaconda. ... I'm going to choose a random number between the first and the nth responder. ... I'm going to say random.randint. And I'm going to choose a random number between 16 and 272, OK. 75. OK. Great. I chose a random number. And I'm going to find the number in the responder's sheet. ... Lauren Z-O-V. Yeah. Nice.\",\n      \"finding\": \"Accurate.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"target\": 3,\n      \"draft_quote\": \"平方根算法说明命令式知识：求 x 的平方根时，从猜测 g 开始（如 x=16、g=3），计算 g*g；若 g*g 足够接近 x，就停止并返回 g；否则把 g 与 x/g 的平均值作为新猜测并重复。\",\n      \"source_quote\": \"To find the square root of a number x, let's say x is originally 16, if a computer follows this algorithm, it's going to start with a guess, g, let's say, 3. We're trying to find the square root of 16. We're going to calculate g times g is 9. And we're going to ask is if g times g is close enough to x, then stop and say, g is the answer. ... If it's not close enough, then I'm going to make a new guess by averaging g and x over g. That's x over g here. And that's the average over there. And the new average is going to be my new guess.\",\n      \"finding\": \"Accurate.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"target\": 3,\n      \"draft_quote\": \"该配方/算法有三部分：简单的步骤序列（四步）、带判断与重复的控制流，以及停止方式（如接近程度取 .01 或 .001）。\",\n      \"source_quote\": \"What were the three parts of the recipe? One was a simple sequence of steps. There were four steps. The other was a flow of control, so there were parts where we made decisions. Are we close enough? There were parts where we repeated some steps. At the end, we said, repeat steps 1, 2, 3. That's the flow of control. And the last part of the recipe was a way to stop. ... In the previous example, the way of stopping was that we decided we were close enough. Close enough was maybe being within .01, .001, whatever you pick.\",\n      \"finding\": \"Accurate.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"target\": 4,\n      \"draft_quote\": \"历史上，固定程序计算机（如计算器）只懂加、减、乘、除，不能绘图、发邮件或上网，要做另一件事就得另造一台固定程序机。\",\n      \"source_quote\": \"Originally, there were these things called fixed-program computers. ... But way back then, an example of a fixed-program computer is this calculator. It only knows how to do addition, multiplication, subtraction, division. If you want to plot something, you can't. If you want to go on the internet, send email with it, you can't. It can only do this one thing. And if you wanted to create a machine that did another thing, then you'd have to create another fixed-program computer that did a completely separate test.\",\n      \"finding\": \"Accurate.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"target\": 4,\n      \"draft_quote\": \"存储程序计算机能存储一段指令序列、执行它，并能改变序列来执行不同指令，从而在同一台机器上做不同任务；这就是今天的计算机。\",\n      \"source_quote\": \"That's when stored-program computers came into play. And these were machines that could store a sequence of instructions. And these machines could execute the sequence of instructions. And you could change the sequence of instructions and execute this different sequence of instructions. You could do different tasks in the same machine. And that's the computer as we know it these days.\",\n      \"finding\": \"Accurate.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"target\": 5,\n      \"draft_quote\": \"每台计算机核心的基本机器架构有四个主要部分：内存、输入/输出、算术逻辑单元（ALU）和控制单元。\",\n      \"source_quote\": \"The basic machine architecture-- at the heart of every computer there's just this basic architecture-- and it contains, I guess, four main parts. The first is the memory. Input and output is the other one. The ALU is where all of the operations are done. ... Interacting with the Arithmetic Logic Unit is the Control Unit.\",\n      \"finding\": \"Accurate.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"target\": 5,\n      \"draft_quote\": \"内存保存数据和指令序列；ALU 执行加、减等原始操作；控制单元含一个程序计数器，从第一条指令开始，把指令送给 ALU，通常递增 1 到下一指令。\",\n      \"source_quote\": \"What the memory contains is a bunch of data and your sequence of instructions. ... The ALU is where all of the operations are done. And the operations that the ALU can do are really primitive operations, addition, subtraction, and so on. ... And the Control Unit contains one program counter. When you load a sequence of instructions, the program counter starts at the first sequence. It starts at the sequence, at the first instruction. It gets what the instruction is, and it sends it to the ALU. ... And after it's done, the ALU is going to go back, and the program counter is going to increase by 1, which means that we're going to go to the next sequence in the instruction set.\",\n      \"finding\": \"Accurate.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"target\": 5,\n      \"draft_quote\": \"测试指令返回真或假，并可能决定下一条指令或跳转（例如把程序计数器设回开头）；执行最后一条指令后可能输出结果。\",\n      \"source_quote\": \"There might be one particular instruction that does some sort of test. It's going to say, is this particular value greater or equal to or the same as this other particular value? That's a test, an example of a test. And the test is going to either return true or false. And depending on the result of that test, you might either go to the next instruction, or you might set the program counter to go all the way back to the beginning, and so on. ... And after you're done, when you finished executing the last instruction, then you might output something.\",\n      \"finding\": \"Accurate.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"target\": 5,\n      \"draft_quote\": \"解释器逐条经过指令，决定下一条、跳过或重复指令。\",\n      \"source_quote\": \"And the interpreter goes through every instruction and decides whether you're going to go to the next instruction, skip instructions, or repeat instructions, and so on.\",\n      \"finding\": \"Accurate.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"target\": 6,\n      \"draft_quote\": \"原始操作包括加、减、逻辑操作、测试（等于、小于）以及移动/存储数据。\",\n      \"source_quote\": \"The primitive operations that it can do are addition, subtraction, logic operations, tests-- which are something equal to something else, something less than, and so on-- and moving data, so storing data, moving data around, and things like that.\",\n      \"finding\": \"Accurate.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"target\": 6,\n      \"draft_quote\": \"Alan Turing 表明，用六条原语——move left、move right、read、write、scan、do nothing——和一条纸带可以计算任何东西。\",\n      \"source_quote\": \"Alan Turing, who was a really great computer scientist, he showed that you can compute anything using the six primitives. And the six primitives are move left, move right, read, write, scan, and do nothing. Using those six instructions and the piece of tape, he showed that you can compute anything.\",\n      \"finding\": \"Accurate.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"target\": 6,\n      \"draft_quote\": \"编程语言提供了更方便的原语；一个重要推论是：如果某件事能用 Python 计算，理论上就能用任何其他语言写程序计算完全相同的事。\",\n      \"source_quote\": \"And using those six instructions, programming languages came about that created a more convenient set of primitives. You don't have to program in only these six commands. And one interesting thing, or one really important thing, that came about from these six primitives is that if you can compute something in Python, let's say-- if you write a program that computes something in Python, then, in theory, you can write a program that computes the exact same thing in any other language.\",\n      \"finding\": \"Accurate.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"target\": 7,\n      \"draft_quote\": \"语言有了原语后可以创建表达式；表达式是原语的组合，有值和意义。\",\n      \"source_quote\": \"Once you have your set of primitives for a particular language, you can start creating expressions. And these expressions are going to be combinations of the primitives in the programming language. And the expressions are going to have some value. And they're going up some meaning in the programming language.\",\n      \"finding\": \"Accurate.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"target\": 7,\n      \"draft_quote\": \"Python 的原始构造包括 float、Boolean、数字、字符串和简单运算符（加、减等）。\",\n      \"source_quote\": \"Programming languages-- in Python, there are primitives, but there aren't as many of them. There are floats, Booleans, these are numbers, strings, and simple operators, like addition, subtraction, and so on.\",\n      \"finding\": \"Accurate.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"target\": 7,\n      \"draft_quote\": \"英语类比中，词是原始构造；“cat, dog, boy”（名词、名词、名词）语法无效，而“cat hugs boy”（名词、动词、名词）语法有效；类似地，Python 中一个词后跟数字 5 语法无效，而 operator、operand、operator 可以。\",\n      \"source_quote\": \"In English, the primitive constructs are going to be words. ... In English, we can say something like, \\\"cat, dog, boy. That, we say, is not syntactically valid. That's bad syntax. That's noun, noun, noun. That doesn't make sense. What does have good syntax in English is noun, verb, noun. So, \\\"cat, hugs boy\\\" is syntactically valid. Similarly, in a programming language, something like this-- in Python, in this case-- a word and then the number five doesn't really make sense. It's not syntactically valid. But something like operator, operand, operator is OK.\",\n      \"finding\": \"Accurate.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"target\": 8,\n      \"draft_quote\": \"英语中语法正确的表达可以有多个含义，例如“flying planes can be dangerous”可指飞行飞机的行为危险或空中的飞机危险，“This reading lamp hasn't uttered a word since I bought it”玩“reading lamp”的双关；但程序只有一种含义，只是可能不是程序员意图的含义。\",\n      \"source_quote\": \"In English, you can actually have more than one meaning to an entire phrase. In this case, \\\"flying planes can be dangerous\\\" can have two meanings. It's the act of flying a plane is dangerous, or the plane that is in the air is dangerous. And this might be a cuter example. \\\"This reading lamp hasn't uttered a word since I bought it. What's going on?\\\" So that has two meanings. It's playing on the word \\\"reading lamp.\\\" That's in English. In English, you can have a sentence that has more than one meaning, that's syntactically correct and static, semantically correct. But in programming languages, the program that you write, the set of instructions that you write, only has one meaning. ... But the problem that comes into play in programming languages is",
